  "prediction": "This report provides an overview of cybersecurity-related bills in the 115th and 116th Congresses, as well as related hearings. In the 116th Congress, the House has passed four cybersecurity bills and the Senate has passed one. A total of 31 bills were passed in the 115th Congress. Notable bills include the National Defense Authorization Acts for Fiscal Years 2018 and 2019, which established various cybersecurity initiatives, and the FITAR Act of 2017, which requires agencies to address IT investments and cybersecurity risks. The report also lists cybersecurity hearings in the 116th Congress, but notes that the list is not definitive. Tables in the report provide links to specific hearings on the respective committee websites. Overall, the report highlights the legislative efforts to address cybersecurity challenges in the US.",
  "scored_prediction": "This report provides an overview of cybersecurity-related bills in the 115th and 116th Congresses, as well as related hearings. In the 116th Congress, the House has passed four cybersecurity bills and the Senate has passed one. A total of 31 bills were passed in the 115th Congress. Notable bills include the National Defense Authorization Acts for Fiscal Years 2018 and 2019, which established various cybersecurity initiatives, and the FITAR Act of 2017, which requires agencies to address IT investments and cybersecurity risks. The report also lists cybersecurity hearings in the 116th Congress, but notes that the list is not definitive. Tables in the report provide links to specific hearings on the respective committee websites. Overall, the report highlights the legislative efforts to address cybersecurity challenges in the US.",
  "score": 28.571428072777877,
  "answers": [
    "Most major cybersecurity legislative provisions were enacted prior to 2002, despite many recommendations having been made over the past decade. More recently, in the 115th and 116th Congresses, cybersecurity legislation has received either committee or floor action or final passage, and both chambers have held multiple hearings. In the 116th Congress, a number of House and Senate bills have received consideration, and hearings have been held by committees in each chamber. In the 115th Congress, 31 bills received some type of action (committee consideration or passage by one or both chambers). Five bills became public law. The House held 54 hearings on cybersecurity issues and the Senate held 40 hearings."
  ],
